## INTRODUCTION
Electronic excitations in molecular assemblies of optically active molecules are of fundamental interest in the field of optoelectronic applications.1 Since intermolecular interactions dominate the photo-physical response in the crystalline phase, optical properties such as exciton lifetime, delocalization and migration can specifically be tuned by the packing arrangement of chromophoric building blocks.2 At the same time, aggregates of selfassembled chromophores similarly give rise to large optical non-linearities, which hold great promise in optical and quantum information technologies.3 Therefore, the directed assembly of chromophores in the solid state is desirable and has proved to be challenging in the past.
In this regard, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)4 appear as new materials for photonic applications,5 due to their modular building principle (organic linkers and metallic nodes), which allows for vast opportunities of material design. Consequently, in addition to the traditional research areas like catalysis,6 gas storage and separation,7 MOFs (and related crystalline coordination polymers, CPs) have also been investigated towards possible applications in optoelectronics8 or non-linear optics (NLO).9 The use of chromophore molecules as linkers allows for their controlled spatial arrangement in the solid state and in turn for accessing excitonic coupling by use of reticular chemistry principles (size of the linker, connectivity and topology).4b, 10 Consequently, crystalline coordination networks of chromophores constitute an ideal platform for the  development of molecular solid state materials holding specific photophysical properties such as efficient exciton hopping,11 anisotropic energy transfer,8g, 12 amplified stimulated emission13 or high absorption efficiencies.9c
Multi-photon absorption (MPA), is a NLO up-conversion process playing a key role in the fields of high density data storage, optical limiting/lasing or bioimaging and is therefore in the focus of current material research.9g, 14 In multichromophoric aggregates and solid-state materials several mechanism for enhanced MPA response have been discussed, e.g. enhancement via increased conjugation between sub-units (“through-bond”) or via intermolecular (excitonic) interactions between single chromophores (“through-space”).3c The latter is focus of recent research, as enhancing the NLO response as a result of aggregation can open new ways for the realization of efficient MPA absorbers.3d, 3e, 3f Cooperative effects elicited by the close proximity of ligand molecules are also considered to be responsible for strong NLO response in MOFs, however, still lack experimental evidence.9g Directed intermolecular interactions in MOFs and CPs can constitute an efficient way to tune their NLO response.
For instance, we recently demonstrated MPA with exceptionally high cross-section values in a series of tetrakisphenyl-ethylene based MOFs, as a function of the topological network and changes in the local linker conformation.9c
In this work, we employ triphenylamine derived linkers as building blocks $\mathrm{(H_{4}T P B D)}$ , which are A-π-D push-pull type ligands $\mathbf{\hat{A}}\ =$ acceptor, $\mathrm{~D~=~donor})$ , facilitating a strong intramolecular charge transfer (Scheme 1).15 The centrosymmetric multipolar geometry, featuring a phenylene diamine core (D) and four biphenyl carboxylic acid groups (A) leads to an enhanced polarizability15-16 a prerequisite for strong NLO response. $\mathrm{Zn(II)}$ and Cd(II) as $\mathbf{d}^{\scriptscriptstyle10}$ -metal ions were used to exclude a potential involvement of the nodes in the photophysical processes. Two new crystalline frameworks were synthesized under solvothermal conditions and subsequently intensively characterized towards their structural parameters and non-linear absorption. The results were then analyzed to identify a potential structure-property relationship including possible existing cooperative enhancement effects (“through-space”) towards MPA in the coordination polymers, aiming to understand the underlying mechanisms and trying to unravel effects in the NLO response of chromophore coordination networks.
## RESULTS and DISCUSSION
Synthesis and Characterization. The push-pull chromophore tetrakis[ $\bf(1,1^{'}$ -biphenyl)- $^{\cdot}4$ -carboxylic acid]- 1,4-benzene diamine $\mathrm{(H_{4}T P B D}$ , Scheme 1) was synthesized in a three-step reaction procedure starting from $\mathrm{N}^{\prime},\mathrm{N}^{\prime\prime},\mathrm{N}^{\prime\prime},\mathrm{N}^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetraphenylbenzene- $^{\cdot1,4}$ -diamine.17 The synthetic procedure, structural and characterization details are provided in the ESI. The reaction of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixture of dimethylacetamide $\mathrm{(DMAc)/H_{2}O}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ yielded pale yellow $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray quality single crystals of a fluorescent two-dimensional framework with the composition $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(T P B D)(D M A c)_{2}]}}$ (tPPD-Zn, Figures 1 and S14 to $S_{17}$ , see ESI for details on structure elucidation). The 2- dimensional framework crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $\boldsymbol{P}\boldsymbol{2}_{\mathrm{1}}/c$ and is composed of a four connecting $\left(4^{-\mathbf{c}}\right)$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ node (Figure 1c) and the ligand TPBD (Figure 1b and Scheme 1). The secondary building unit (SBU) contains two zinc atoms which are connected by four TPBD ligand motifs and two DMAc molecules. Each zinc atom is penta-coordinated in a distorted square pyramidal coordination environment consisting of three carboxylate groups and one DMAc solvent molecule. One carboxylate group coordinates in a bidentate asymmetrical chelating fashion, whereas the two other carboxylic groups bridge the two zinc atoms in a $\kappa^{\mathrm{1}}\mathbf{:}\kappa^{\mathrm{1}}$ mode, leading to a rhombical node structure (Figure 1c). Topology analysis of tPPD-Zn using the TOPOSpro18 software package classified the materials as a twofold interpenetrated uninodal $(4,4)$ -network of sql topology (Figures 1d and S15). The compound adopts a herringbone type packing of the TPBD ligand within the crystallographic bc-plane with stacking along the $b$ and $c-$ axis, respectively (Figures 4c and S17). The stacking molecules show coplanar ordering with distances of 10.0 Å and 15.1 Å (center-to-center distance of the pphenylenediamine cores). The phase purity and stability against different solvents as well as long-term stability of the bulk tPPD-Zn was investigated by powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD, Figures $S_{7}-S_{9}\rangle$ ) matching the results from single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (SCXRD) analysis. The compound is thermally stable up to 440 $^{\circ}C$ (thermogravimetric analysis, TGA; Figure $S_{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ). Infrared (IR) and Raman spectroscopy show the expected bands for coordinated TPBD (Figures S23 and $S_{24}$ ).
The reaction of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ with $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ under similar solvothermal conditions as for tPPD-Zn, yielded pale yellow crystals of a fluorescent three-dimensional framework with the composition $\mathrm{[Cd_{2}(T P B D)(H_{2}O)_{4}]}$ (tPPD-Cd, Figures 2 and S18 to S21). Single crystals suitable for X-ray structure analysis were obtained directly from the synthesis (see ESI for details on structure elucidation and synthetic procedure). The three-dimensional framework tPPD-Cd crystallizes in the triclinic space group $P\bar{1}$ and is built up by a four connecting $\left(4^{-\mathbf{C}}\right)$ ${\mathrm{Cd}}^{2+}$ node unit and the ligand TPBD (Figure 2b and 2c). The node contains two cadmium atoms which are coordinated by four carboxylate groups and four water molecules. One $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ cation is hexacoordinated in a bidendate  fashion by two carboxylate and two water molecules. The cadmium atoms are bridged by two carboxylates. Interestingly, the rhombic node of tPPD-Zn is resembled in tPPD-Cd (Figure 2c). Topology analysis revealed a threefold interpenetrated uninodal $(4,4)$ -network showing cds topology (Figure 2d and S19). Like in the monoclinic tPPD-Zn, the triclinic tPPD-Cd adopts a herringbone type packing within the crystallographic $a b$ -plane with stacking along the $a$ and $b$ - axis (Figure 4d and Figure S21). The chromophores of tPPD-Cd show co-planar stacking with a center-to-center distance of 11.5 Å and 10.9 Å. The phase purity, solvent and long-term stability of the bulk material was confirmed by PXRD and TGA measurements (Figures S10 – S12, and S6). Comparison of the IR and Raman spectra with the free ligand $\mathrm{H_{4}T P B D}$ showed the characteristic bands, proofing the successful incorporation of the linker in the Cd-MOF (Figures $S_{23}$ and $S24$ ). Thermal stability was proofed by TGA measurements, which revealed thermal stability up to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S6).
The reaction of the four connecting linker $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ with group-12 metal ions yields structurally closely related uninodal $^{(4,4)}$ -networks, however the reaction with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ delivers a regular 2D-net (sql, Figure S15) whereas the reaction with $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ gives a $\ensuremath{3\mathrm{D}}$ rod-net (cds, Figure S19). The in-situ formed metal-oxo bimetallic nodes show a rhombic-planar symmetry (Figures 1c, 2c, S14 and S18). Both network compounds show different grades of interpenetration, two-fold for tPPD-Zn (Figure 1e) and three-fold for tPPD-Cd (Figure 2e). Importantly, the local ligand distortion of TPBD in tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd caused by the coordination bond formation and the framework integration is similar, as proofed by Raman spectroscopy and by comparison of molecular structures from SCXRD (Figure S22). Although both materials hold different underlying topologies, the coordination networks show the same herringbone arrangements of the chromophores, however with a quite significantly shorter distance between co-planar TPBD molecules in tPPD-Cd $(\sim24~\%$ reduction along the a-axis, Figure 4c and 4d) engendered by the higher grade of network interpenetration. This specific structural characteristic of the chromophore packing will result in a pronounced difference in the linear and non-linear optical properties of the network compounds, as we show in the following sections. Consequently, network interpenetration in MOFs should be considered as a further tool for tuning the MPA properties of this material class.
Figure 2. a) A portion of the single crystal structure of tPPD-Cd; b) Representation of the four connecting ligand motif TPBD; c) The ${\mathrm{Cd}}^{2+}$ containing SBU (oxygen $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ red, nitrogen $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ blue, carbon $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ grey, cadmium $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ green); d) The underlying network topology of tPPD-Cd; e) Interpenetration of the three cds networks.
The absorption  and reflectance spectra of tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd show a distinct gas-to-crystal shift compared to $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ with the absorption edge located at \~ 483 nm (tPPD-Zn) and $\sim506~\mathrm{nm}$ (tPPD-Cd, Figures $S62\ensuremath{\mathrm{~-~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S67}},$ ). The PL spectra show emission centered at $\sim520\ \mathrm{nm}$ for tPPD-Zn and \~ 521 nm for tPPD-Cd $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}~=~400~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , Figures S31 and $S_{35}\mathrm{\overline{{}}}$ ). The determined external quantum yields amount to $\Phi({\bf t P P D-Z n})=0.24$ and $\Phi({\bf t P P D-C d})=$ 0.52. The PL spectra of these compounds were also analyzed using high fluence femto-second laser pulses as excitation with a repetition rate of $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{1}}\operatorname{kHz}$ . In this case, the PL intensities were recorded using a fiber-coupled CCD spectrometer. Due to the higher resolution of this detector, the femtosecond laser excited photoluminescence showed several well-resolved peaks in the emission of tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd, which were deconvoluted in its single fractions using gaussian distribution functions (Figures $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{33}$ and $\mathrm{s}_{37}\mathrm{,}$ ). The assignment and fitting of the contributions to a vibronic progression of high frequency phenyl/ring stretching modes $\left(\sim1600~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\right)$ ) show good agreement between the calculated and measured PL spectra (see the ESI for details on the vibronic analysis). We like to note that such features are common in absorption and emission spectra of organic conjugated compounds.1c To further investigate the origin of the PL we also conducted PLE measurements, focusing on the maximum and a lower and higher wavelength within the emission range, see arrows in Figure $^3$ . Like in the case of the higher concentrated ligand solutions $\left(10^{-3}\mathrm{\mol/L}\right.$ , Figure $^3$ and S28) we observed an additional red-shifted band centered at \~ 450 nm. This implies that similarly to the linker in higher concentrated solutions, also in the coordination networks the emission originates from various feeding states over the whole emission range holding excitonic character. Both compounds adopt a herringbone type packing with two molecules TPBD per unit cell, which would allow for Coulombic excitonic coupling within the weak exciton-coupling regime.21 For a characteristic dimeric chromophore structure, if non-coplanar aligned transition dipole moments (TDMs) of two translationally invariant molecules in a primitive unit cell couple, transitions to the upper and lower dimer components are allowed simultaneously.21b Note that in the present case, the absorption band of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ is composed of two orthogonal transitions according to TD-DFT (TDMs along the molecular x and y-axis, Scheme 1), which would lead to six possible coupling modes (Figure S61). By applying a simple dipole-dipole interaction model for herringbone type dimers as a first approximation,22 we calculated the expected splitting energies $\mathrm{V_{dipole}}$ based on geometric parameters extracted from the SC-XRD structures (Figures 4a and $\mathrm{\sfS}_{59}$ , S60, Table $\mathbf{S12}$ ,  a detailed analysis of exciton coupling is given in the ESI). The calculated splitting energies are in good accordance with the measured values from excitation, reflectance and absorption spectra, reproducing the found red shifts compared to the single ligand chromophore. We further conducted a series of TD-DFT calculations on several TPBD-clusters (dimer and trimer structure aggregates) extracted from the respective crystal-structures (see ESI for details). Excited state properties of the TPBD-clusters were assessed by simulation of the electronic absorption spectra and by analyzing atomic centered transition density matrices. The latter reveals weakly bound polar excitonic states5l with an exciton radius of up to three chromophore units. The excitonic analysis applying dipole-dipole coupling is recovered in the TD-DFT calculations, however, the simulations demonstrate that intermolecular interactions in the coordination network materials will go beyond pure electrostatic interaction and charge separated states will also influence the photoresponse (see the discussion in the ESI).
To sum up, the previous analysis suggest the existence of excitonically coupled states with strong delocalization and distinct charge-transfer characteristics. Such excitonic interactions in chromophore assemblies have been discussed as an enhancement mechanism to strong cooperative NLO response,3a, 3c, 23 and we consequently expect a pronounced structure relation towards the TPA response of tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd. To proof this working hypothesis, we focused on their non-linear absorption properties namely the two-photon excited fluorescence (TPEF) spectra in a spectral region close to that of one-photon resonance.
Two-photon Absorption Induced Photoluminescence.  Below-band-gap optical excitation of tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd was studied using an excitation source at $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The excitation energy for twophoton absorption (TPA) was provided by a femtosecond laser with 100 fs pulses with a repetition rate of 1 kHz. We observe a PL centered around $\sim520\ \mathrm{nm}$ , with no shift compared to the measured linear PL (Figure 5a and ${\displaystyle5\mathrm{b}}.$ ). Since linear absorption at the excitation wavelength is negligible, the absorption process is of non-linear origin, indicating a TPA process. The absence of a real accessible energy level at $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ shows that the absorption process must proceed via a virtual state, which excludes resonance enhancement effects in the TPA properties. Interestingly, we observe the same vibronic replicas as in the linear case (discussedabove), showing that the nonlinear absorption in the network compounds proceeds via linker centered states. To gain information about the order of the NLO absorption, power-dependent measurements were carried out. Since the probability of NLO absorption is a power function of the excitation fluence, the intensity of the luminescence follows a power law. Thus, by measuring the PL intensity as a function of excitation intensity, one can obtain the order of the NLO absorption. We find that a power-two process takes place while exciting at 800 nm identifying TPA as the underlying NLO process (Figure 5). The network compounds show stability against high-power illumination with excitation fluences in the $\mathrm{{mJ/cm^{2}}}$ region, however, with clear signs of photo-saturation.
We now turn our attention to the structure-property relation of TPA in the two coordination networks. A relative estimate about the enhancement of the TPA properties of tPPD-Zn and tPPD-Cd can be obtained by the comparison of their integrated TPEF areas at similar excitation powers (equation 1).
$$
\frac{\sigma_{t P P D-C d}^{T P A}}{\sigma_{t P P D-Z n}^{T P A}}=\xi=\frac{A_{t P P D-C d}\cdot d_{t P P D-Z n}\cdot\varphi_{t P P D-Z n}}{A_{t P P D-Z n}\cdot d_{t P P D-C d}\cdot\varphi_{t P P D-C d}}
$$
where $\sigma$ is the TPA cross-section, A is the integrated TPEF area normalized by the integration time, d is the chromophore density (number of linkers per unit cell) and $\varphi$ is the quantum efficiency of the respective network compounds. By using the solid-state TPEF method9g, 24, we found an enhancement factor of $\xi=9$ for the TPA cross-sections of tPPD-Cd compared to tPPD-Zn (Figure 6b, Table $S_{5}$ and equation 1). Key elements for enhanced two-photon absorptivity in aggregates and solid-state materials have been described to originate from an increase in the number of active centers and by coherent interactions between the single chromophores via conjugation (“through bond”) or “through-space” (excitonic) interactions.3c Both CPs, tPPD-Zn and tPPDCd exhibit slightly different chromophore densities (tPPD-Zn: 1.00 $\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{d}\mathrm{m}^{3}$ , tPPD-Cd: 1.19 $\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{d}\mathrm{m}^{3})$ ) and therefore we would only expect slightly higher TPEF areas for tPPD-Cd, provided that no intermolecular interactions among neighboring chromophores are existing and the NLO response is only limited by the number of active centers. The higher grade of network interpenetration in tPPD-Cd results in lower distances of neighboring TPBD molecules (decrease of $\sim24\ \%$ for coplanar TPBD stacking, Figure 4c and 4d). This fact seems to dramatically affect the TPA properties of the two CPs. Apparently, the difference in the electronic structure, being a function of the local chromophore arrangement alters the NLO response. TPA scaling via excitonic coupling only takes place in a spectral region close to one-photon resonance, because of the respective exciton splitting energies $\mathrm{(V~\approx~1000~~cm^{-1}}$ , Figure 4). Excitation at $800\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength corresponds to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ absorption, which is close to the absorption band of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ $\left(372\mathrm{~\nm}\right)$ . Consequently, band-gab states of excitonic character are addressed during two-photon excitation (two-photon resonance with excitonic states, Figure 4). Furthermore, the PLE analysis of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{4}T P B D}$ (Figure $S26-S28)$ and the CPs as well as the DFT results suggest that these states hold J-type character $(\sim450\ \mathrm{nm}$ ,
Figure $^{3\mathrm{b}}$ ) and are more pronounced in the case of tPPDCd, which shows an enhanced TPA response. TPA scaling in J-type aggregates has been reported for porphyrins or pseudoisocyanine dyes to occur via coherent exciton delocalization.25 In contrast, even though exhibiting the same principle packing motif, in tPPD-Zn the chromophore arrangement is different, which seems to negatively affect the TPA properties. We address this fact to a conceivably higher exciton coherence in tPPD-Cd. In summary, higher chromophore density, denser packing and stronger excitonic interactions cooperatively achieve a significant TPA enhancement in tPPD-Cd compared to tPPD-Zn by approximately one order of magnitude. (Figure 6, details on TPA analysis in the ESI). We thus argue, that the observed increase in the two-photon response is related to a predominantly “through-space” enhancement mechanism, induced by tighter and specifically packed chromophores as a direct consequence of a higher grade of network interpenetration within tPPD-Cd. The recent results, although not fully conclusive, consolidate that excitonic coupling can constitutes an active mechanism in crystalline coordination polymers for the enhancement of the twophoton absorptivity by excitation in a spectral region close to one-photon resonance.
## CONCLUSIONS
We synthesized and structurally characterized two new fluorescent crystalline network compounds, namely a zinc containing two-dimensional coordination polymer tPPDZn and a cadmium based three-dimensional metalorganic framework tPPD-Cd, using a phenylene diamine derived chromophore as a four-connecting ligand motif. Both materials possess uninodal (4,4)-network topologies (sql and cds) and show herringbone type packing motifs of the chromophore. By using static spectroscopic techniques, we investigated the material towards their two-photon absorptivity and were able to show that a structure-related enhancement of the relative TPA crosssection is most probably associated to an enhanced “through-space” interaction in tPPD-Cd, which is a direct consequence of closer and specifically arranged chromophoric linker molecules.
The present study enabled us to derive quantitative information about the influence of packing density and chromophore arrangement on two-photon absorption properties of photo-active metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and related materials. We used established excited state theories of molecular crystals and aggregates to describe the photophysical response and correlated the findings with the TPA properties of the network compounds. Further studies extending the scope and the number of the investigated systems will be required, to get a more complete understanding of the relevant phenomena. Importantly, the chromophore arrangement in dense (interpenetrated), typically less porous or even non-porous coordination network compounds (MOFs and CPs) tuned out to be a key factor for the control of the NLO properties of these materials. With ongoing understanding of network interpenetration, the entwining of multiple layers can be regarded as a further tool for the rational design of high MPA active coordination networks. Multichromophoric arrangements in MOFs and CPs oriented in a J-type assembly26 (face-totail arrangement) seems to be an interesting target structure for enhanced NLO response. Our results already demonstrate that such specific design of chromophore assemblies in crystalline network materials bears the potential to control the MPA properties, which ultimately can lead to superior MPA materials compared to existing materials and open new avenues for future work in this area, with perspectives ranging from bioimaging (e.g. MPA active MOF nano-particles) to photonic applications (e.g. all-optical switches in MOF nano-crystal waveguides).
